## THE FULL ENCLOSING FUNCTION AFTER THE EDIT — comments stripped, every arm and the common tail

```c
DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc_altstatus)
{
	struct wdc_data *d = (struct wdc_data *) extra;
	uint64_t idata = 0, odata = 0;
	idata = data[0];
	odata = status_byte(d, cpu);              /* UNCONDITIONAL, unchanged */
	if (writeflag==MEM_READ) {
		if (relative_addr != 0) {
			odata = 0;                        /* <-- #525 */
		} else
			debug("[ wdc: read from ALTSTATUS: 0x%02x ]\n", (int)odata);
	} else if (relative_addr != 0) {
		debug("[ wdc: write to DRIVE ADDRESS (read-only): 0x%02x, ignored ]\n", (int)idata);
	} else {
		debug("[ wdc: write to ALT. CTRL: 0x%02x ]\n", (int)idata);
		if (idata & WDCTL_RST) {
			d->cur_command = COMMAND_RESET;
			wdc_new_command_reset(d);
		}
	}
	if (writeflag == MEM_READ)
		data[0] = odata;
	return 1;
}
```

## SETTLED PREMISES

* **The read path never decoded its offset.** `relative_addr` was tested in exactly ONE place, inside
  the `else` of `writeflag == MEM_READ`, so a READ of offset 1 (Drive Address, `0x3f7` on the PC
  layout) returned the ALT-STATUS byte.
* **This is the symmetric cell of `#494`**, which closed the WRITE half of the same `if`. The
  function's inputs give {READ, WRITE} × {offset 0, offset 1}; three cells were closed.
* **Measured, rung 3, committed build:** 14/16 pre-fix with both offset-1 read rows returning the
  status byte (`0x51` power-on, `0x58` armed); **16/16 after**. `M_NULL_EFFECT` (guard kept,
  `odata = 0` deleted) and `M_REINSTATE` both fail the two CORE rows; `M_NULL_MEDIUM` fails a
  DIFFERENT pair (the two ACCEPT rows). Write cells unregressed on every leg.
* **S is nonzero** — `d->error = 1` at devinit gives `WDCS_DRDY|WDCS_DSC|WDCS_ERR = 0x51` — which is
  what makes the clause-(d) mutant non-vacuous.
* **Zero is an emulator policy, not a hardware claim** (`#95` pre-zeroes registered-device read
  buffers). Ownership of `0x3f7` stays open as a separate row.
* **Owner ruling: decode the read; do NOT narrow the registration** (which is still `2` bytes wide).

