624:			    this `if' -- so a READ of offset 1, the Drive Address
625:			    register (0x3f7 on the PC layout), returned the
626:			    ALT-STATUS byte, which matches no hardware on any
627:			    layout.
628:
629:			    THIS IS THE SYMMETRIC CELL OF #494, WHICH CLOSED THE
630:			    WRITE HALF OF THE SAME `if' AND LEFT THIS ONE OPEN.
631:			    The function's own inputs give a two-by-two --
632:			    {READ, WRITE} x {offset 0, offset 1} -- and three
633:			    cells were closed.  Measured at rung 3 on the
634:			    committed build: 14/16 pre-fix with both offset-1
635:			    read rows returning the status byte (0x51 at power-on,
636:			    0x58 armed), 16/16 after.
637:
638:			    ZERO IS AN EMULATOR POLICY, NOT A HARDWARE CLAIM.
639:			    DC21285-era PC parts leave 0x3f7 to the floppy
640:			    controller -- wdcreg.h compiles wd_digin out under an
641:			    `#if 0' reading "NOT MAPPED; fd uses this register on
642:			    PCs", and dev_fdc.c's DEV_FDC_LENGTH carries "TODO 8,
643:			    but collision with wdc".  Whose register it really is
644:			    stays open (row wdcaltownership).  What is settled is
645:			    that it is not ours to answer with our status byte, so
646:			    the declined read returns the deterministic 0 that #95
647:			    already pre-zeroes the caller's buffer with, rather
648:			    than inventing a value.
649:
650:			    SILENT, like #494's write decline and for the same
651:			    reason: a correct guest reads here expecting a FLOPPY
652:			    effect, so a wdc diagnostic would report correct
653:			    behaviour as a fault.
654:
655:			    THE REGISTRATION IS DELIBERATELY NOT NARROWED.  A
656:			    2 -> 1 at the memory_device_register() below would
657:			    close both offset-1 cells structurally, but it moves
658:			    0x3f7 from HANDLED-AND-DECLINED to UNMAPPED -- a
659:			    different path whose behaviour here is unmeasured --
660:			    and it would leave #494's arm as dead code carrying a
661:			    measured comment.  Owner ruling: decode the read.  */
662:			odata = 0;
663:		} else
664:			debug("[ wdc: read from ALTSTATUS: 0x%02x ]\n",
665:			    (int)odata);
666:	} else if (relative_addr != 0) {
667:		/*  #494: THE HANDLER NEVER DECODED ITS OWN OFFSET.  The region is
668:		    registered TWO BYTES wide, so offset 1 -- the Drive Address
669:		    register, 0x3f7 on the PC layout -- arrived here and was treated
670:		    as a Device Control write.  Since #481 corrected the reset bit,
671:		    that meant a guest store to a READ-ONLY register aborted a live
672:		    ATA write and purged the queued ring.  Measured at rung 3 on an
673:		    unmodified evbmips -e malta; the witness is preserved under
674:		    regress/artifacts/wdcaltoffset/witness_20260829/.
675:
676:		    THE WHOLE ACTION IS DECLINED, NOT JUST THE HELPER CALL, AND THAT
677:		    IS THE POINT.  The reset has two effects with two owners: the
678:		    cur_command assignment below, and the ring/DRQ clearing inside
679:		    wdc_new_command_reset().  A fix that guards only the helper leaves
680:		    the ATAPI signature latched.  That was MEASURED, not reasoned: it
681:		    escapes the witness AND the detector that replaced it with a clean
682:		    sweep, because every row in both observes DRQ or the ring and
683:		    neither varies under it.  The kill table and its scores are under
684:		    regress/artifacts/wdcaltoffset/killtable_20260830/ -- read TABLE.txt
685:		    for the counts rather than trusting a number written here, which is
686:		    how this sentence was wrong once already.
687:
688:		    SILENT BY DESIGN, at debug() level and with no latch.  The house
689:		    idiom announces a guest ANOMALY, and this is not one: the tree
690:		    records in two places that this byte belongs to the floppy
691:		    controller on PCs -- wdcreg.h compiles wd_digin out under
692:		    an `#if 0` whose own note reads "NOT MAPPED; fd uses this
693:		    register on PCs" (the delimiters are dropped here on purpose:
694:		    a comment terminator quoted inside a comment ends it), and
695:		    dev_fdc.c's DEV_FDC_LENGTH carries `TODO 8, but collision with
696:		    wdc`.  A correct guest writes here expecting a floppy effect, so
697:		    a wdc diagnostic would report correct behaviour as a fault.
698:
699:		    THE ACCESS STAYS HANDLED (return 1), AND THE REASON IS NOT THE ONE
700:		    THIS COMMENT FIRST GAVE.  It said a 0 would fall through to
701:		    unassigned-address handling and warn per access.  That is false: this
702:		    device is REGISTERED, so a 0 never reaches that path.  memory_rw sets
703:		    res = -1, takes its res <= 0 branch, and raises EXCEPTION_DBE --
704:		    measured, 19793 `device 'wdc_altstatus' ... failed` lines and ZERO
705:		    `non-existant` ones under -v.  The guest then loops forever
706:		    re-executing the faulting store, and at DEFAULT verbosity it does so
707:		    in SILENCE.  That is worse than a warning flood, not better, which is
708:		    why the wrong reason was worth correcting rather than dropping: a
709:		    maintainer who reads `only noisy warnings` may decide a 0 is fine.
710:
711:		    The arm falls through to the common tail, which is a no-op for it --
712:		    the tail only assigns on the READ path.  (An earlier draft justified
713:		    that by the untouched-buffer hazard; #95 already zeroes the caller's
714:		    buffer, so that hazard cannot arise here either.)  */
715:		debug("[ wdc: write to DRIVE ADDRESS (read-only): 0x%02x,"
716:		    " ignored ]\n", (int)idata);
717:	} else {
718:		debug("[ wdc: write to ALT. CTRL: 0x%02x ]\n",
719:		    (int)idata);
720:		/*  #481: THE RESET BIT IS WDCTL_RST (0x04), NOT WDCTL_4BIT (0x08).
721:		
722:		    WDCTL_4BIT is "use four head bits (wd1003)", a head-addressing
723:		    mode selector that Linux libata holds as its STEADY-STATE ap->ctl.
724:		    Testing it here meant three measured defects at once:
725:		
726:		      * a real soft reset was MISSED.  Older OpenBSD asserts with 0x06,
727:		        which has bit 3 CLEAR -- measured `cur_command 0x0`.
728:		      * a ROUTINE control write DESTROYED THE RECORD OF WHICH COMMAND IS
729:		        EXECUTING -- measured `0xec -> 0x100`.  cur_command is also the
730:		        value assigned into write_in_progress, so this is only harmless
731:		        because wdc__write() has exactly ONE caller, immediately after
732:		        the assignment.  An accident, not an invariant.
733:		      * an nIEN write latched a reset.  0x0a is libata's polled-mode
734:		        staple -- measured `0x0a->0x100`.
735:		
736:		    COMMAND_RESET IS NOT A RESET ACTION.  It is read at exactly two
737:		    sites, both making the cylinder registers return the ATAPI signature
738:		    0xEB14 for a CD-ROM.  It is the SIGNATURE LATCH -- which is why the
739:		    wrong bit went unnoticed: triggering on libata's steady state kept
740:		    the signature on almost always.
741:		
742:		    LEVEL-TRIGGERED, DELIBERATELY, AND THE MEASURED DIFFERENCE
743:		    FAVOURS THIS FORM.  A channel held in reset must not carry bytes
744:		    out the far side, and an edge-triggered abort lets it:
745:		
746:		        assert 0x0e, 8 guest stores to DATA, rewrite 0x0e, release
747:		          LEVEL (this form):  occ 0, DRQ 0   -- the ring is purged
748:		          EDGE:               occ 8, DRQ 1   -- the bytes survive the
749:		                                                 whole reset, and the
750:		                                                 status byte still
751:		                                                 advertises DRQ
752:		
753:		    Measured through register writes only, with a liveness control (the
754:		    same traffic without a reset leaves occ 8, so the 0 is the reset
755:		    working and not a dead path).
756:		
757:		    *** AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS COMMENT SAID edge and level were
758:		    "indistinguishable here, because the abort is idempotent and no
759:		    protocol-following driver issues commands while holding SRST".
760:		    THAT WAS MEASURED FALSE.  Three things were wrong with it:
761:		
762:		      * the differential behind it only ever asserted SRST ONCE, and
763:		        the forms differ only when a driver HOLDS it across two
764:		        control writes with something accruing in between;
765:		      * IDEMPOTENCE OF THE HELPER DOES NOT LICENCE THE INFERENCE.
766:		        It means repeating the abort is harmless.  It says nothing
767:		        about SKIPPING the repeat, which is what an edge does, and
768:		        state accrues between the writes;
769:		      * the scenario above ISSUES NO COMMAND.  Plain data-register
770:		        stores suffice -- and in a hardening fork the guest need not
771:		        be protocol-following at all.
772:		
773:		    The decision is unchanged and now rests on evidence that does not
774:		    collapse when someone builds the discriminating case. ***
775:		
776:		    Note also that a prev_ctl update mis-scoped by one brace makes
777:		    every LATER reset be ignored -- a failure this form cannot have --
778:		    but that is a secondary argument, not the reason.
779:		
780:		    THE ABORT IS #479's HELPER, not a second one: a channel reset and a
781:		    new command must not disagree about what gets cleared, and #479
782:		    already reasoned out which latches must SURVIVE (see its comment --
783:		    clearing warned_* would undo #337 and #464).  */
784:		if (idata & WDCTL_RST) {
785:			d->cur_command = COMMAND_RESET;
786:			wdc_new_command_reset(d);
787:		}
788:	}
789:
790:	if (writeflag == MEM_READ)
791:		data[0] = odata;
792:
793:	return 1;
794:}
