1605:			/*  #477: THE DISPATCH KEYS ON THE PHASE IN ADDITION TO THE FLAG.
1606:
1607:			    *** THIS HEADLINE READ "ON THE PHASE, NOT ON THE FLAG" UNTIL
1608:			    2026-08-27, AND THAT WORDING WAS UNSAFE.  The codex seat reasoned
1609:			    on #477's pass 2 that deleting the flag conjunct would sweep the
1610:			    detector; MEASURED, it swept all 52 rows clean. ***
1611:
1612:			    BOTH CONJUNCTS ARE LOAD-BEARING, and the reason is a STALE PHASE.
1613:			    `wdc_command(ATAPI_PKT_CMD)` sets PHASE_CMDOUT; a following
1614:			    `wdc_command(WDCC_WRITE)` clears the flag at the top of that
1615:			    function and arms the write -- and NOTHING RESETS THE PHASE.  The
1616:			    device then sits in exactly the state a phase-only guard cannot
1617:			    tell from a real CDB collection.
1618:
1619:			    Under a phase-only guard the guest's own sector payload reaches
1620:			    occupancy 12 and is issued as a SCSI command.  MEASURED, and worse
1621:			    than the seat predicted: the sector does not merely lose twelve
1622:			    bytes, it NEVER REACHES THE DISK -- `0 w, 0 b` against a wanted
1623:			    one write of 512.  `row_stale_phase_write` pins both halves.
1624:
1625:			    The stale phase is itself a defect; it is filed with
1626:			    `wdcwipclear` / `wdcringnoflush` -- a new command does not reset
1627:			    what the old one left.
1628:
1629:			    `atapi_cmd_in_progress` is set when a packet command arrives and
1630:			    cleared only when the NEXT command does -- so it stays 1 through the
1631:			    whole of DATAIN and past PHASE_COMPLETED.  It conflates two roles: 'a
1632:			    packet command is active' (read at four register sites through
1633:			    completion, and must stay set) and 'I am collecting a 12-byte CDB'
1634:			    (what this test actually wants).
1635:
1636:			    So a guest write during DATAIN that brought ring occupancy to exactly
1637:			    12 re-entered this block, FREED THE LIVE TRANSFER, and issued a SCSI
1638:			    command built from the guest's own returned DATA-IN payload.  MEASURED:
1639:			    `freed 1 live transfer(s)`.
1640:
1641:			    NOT guarding the data-port write instead, and two measuring seats
1642:			    agree: this check runs on EVERY data-port write regardless of what the
1643:			    write did -- the width switch's `default:` arm (len 3 or 8) pushes
1644:			    ZERO bytes and falls straight through to here.  Guarding the pushes
1645:			    was MEASURED WORSE THAN NO FIX: with occupancy already 12 the
1646:			    suppressed push leaves it at 12, so the guard CREATES a free on an
1647:			    input the unfixed code survives.
1648:
1649:			    Nor clearing the flag at completion: that leaves this window untouched
1650:			    (the flag is legitimately 1 here) and breaks the ATAPI handshake --
1651:			    ireason and the byte-count pair are read at PHASE_COMPLETED.  */
1652:			/*  #479: A REJECTED WIDTH MUST NOT DISPATCH EITHER.
1653:
1654:			    `#478` added `badlen_this` and guarded the sector DRAIN with
1655:			    it, and recorded that this finally made `#337`'s `(ignored)`
1656:			    message true.  It did not.  Two seats found it independently
1657:			    on `#478`'s pass 2 -- one by reading, one by measuring a
1658:			    3-byte store allocate an `atapi_st` -- and the row
